To implement "starts with" matching for **Branch Code** and **CGL** search functionality, you need to modify the query logic in your Repository or Service layer. Using a prefix match (text%) instead of a substring match (%text%) ensures that the results are strictly filtered by how the values begin.
As a Technical Lead, I recommend the following implementations based on your current Spring Boot and Oracle architecture:
### 1. Spring Data JPA Repository
If you are using Spring Data JPA method names, change the keyword from Containing to StartingWith.
```java
public interface JournalRequestRepository extends JpaRepository<JournalRequest, Long> {

    // Changes search from %query% to query%
    List<JournalRequest> findByReqBranchCodeStartingWith(String branchCode);

    List<JournalRequest> findByReqCglStartingWith(String cgl);
}

```
### 2. Custom JPQL/SQL Query
If you are using the @Query annotation, update the LIKE clause to place the wildcard only at the end of the parameter.
```java
@Query("SELECT j FROM JournalRequest j WHERE j.reqBranchCode LIKE :branchCode%")
List<JournalRequest> searchByBranchPrefix(@Param("branchCode") String branchCode);

@Query("SELECT j FROM JournalRequest j WHERE j.reqCgl LIKE :cgl%")
List<JournalRequest> searchByCglPrefix(@Param("cgl") String cgl);

```
### 3. Dynamic Filtering (Criteria API / Specification)
If your search bar supports multiple dynamic filters, update your Specification to use the startsWith helper or a trailing wildcard.
```java
public static Specification<JournalRequest> branchStartsWith(String branchCode) {
    return (root, query, cb) -> 
        cb.like(root.get("reqBranchCode"), branchCode + "%");
}

public static Specification<JournalRequest> cglStartsWith(String cgl) {
    return (root, query, cb) -> 
        cb.like(root.get("reqCgl"), cgl + "%");
}

```
### 4. Database Indexing Tip
By switching to "starts with" (LIKE 'text%'), you allow the Oracle database to utilize standard B-tree indexes effectively. A leading wildcard (LIKE '%text') forces a full table scan, whereas a trailing wildcard allows for an **Index Range Scan**, significantly improving search performance on large tables like JOURNAL_REQUEST.
Ensure your composite indexes on REQ_BRANCH_CODE and REQ_CGL are optimized for these leading-edge searches.
